00:00:04.973 --> 00:00:06.140
VALERIYA SHIN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.190
I'm Valeriya Shin, a customer
engineer at Google Cloud.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.440
ERICA CHUONG: And I'm Erica
Chuong, a partner engineer

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.880
at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.520
Welcome back to another segment
of "Google Cloud Technical

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.810
Guides for Startups."

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.020
In this video, we'll be focusing
on Continuous Integration

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.540
and Continuous Deployment,
also known as CI/CD,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.220
using Google Cloud.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.930
In this episode,
we're going to cover,

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.630
what is CI/CD,
CI/CD in practice,

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:32.380
setting up CI/CD, as well as
a Google Cloud Console demo.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.150
So without any
further ado, let's

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.390
get right into exploring the
Google Cloud platform together.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.360
Continuous Integration,
or CI, is the process

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.640
of automating the building
and testing of changes

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.610
to code in order to
detect problems early.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.280
Continuous Delivery,
or CD, is the process

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.530
of automating deployments
across multiple environments

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:53.270
in a safe, low-risk,
and quick manner.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.360
Let's take a deeper
look into each.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.760
CI-- again, Continuous
Integration--

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.950
is the process where
code changes undergo

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:03.160
a series of automated processes
to ensure that the application

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.110
or system is still working.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.500
Part of the CI pipeline
may be to build or compile

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.440
an application once
the code changes have

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.880
been made in order to
validate and ensure that there

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.540
are no breaking changes.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.990
This can be achieved through
a shared repository where

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.710
developers commit and merge code
that is then saved, reviewed,

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.090
and tested.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.710
Additional steps could be
included and automated,

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.730
such as unit tests, integration
tests, and deployment

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.050
of the same artifact.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.870
You should have some sort
of tool for source code

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.780
management, such as Cloud
Source Repositories, GitHub,

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.250
or Bitbucket.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.610
You can also have a CI server
for building and testing code,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.670
such as Cloud Build,
Jenkins, or circleCI.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.250
With continuous delivery,
deploying artifacts

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.430
from your CI server
must be done manually.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.580
With CD, or Continuous
Deployment, on the other hand,

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.760
this is the practice of
delivering new software

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.520
features through an
automated deployment process.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.840
Usually right after CI, CD may
take the build artifact and use

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.940
one of the many different
deployment paradigms

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.560
to deploy the artifact.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.690
Everything is fully
automated, and having

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.449
every single commit to your
source code management system

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.510
built into the artifacts and
then deployed to production.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.550
Some common tools for
continuous deployment

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.700
include Puppet, Jenkins,
and bash scripts.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.070
Now that we've gone through
what continuous integration

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.050
and continuous
deployment are, you

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:25.570
may be wondering why they're
such important concepts

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:26.500
to apply.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.750
First, they ensure that
all changes to code

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.540
are tracked, tested,
and built. Documentation

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.580
is key so there are no
questions as to what

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.580
features are in each release
and who made the changes.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:39.220
Second, automating a lot
of these manual tasks

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.900
reduces chances of human error.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.090
Going back to the
importance of documentation,

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.980
should there be
any sort of bugs,

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.320
these will be easily
trackable and you can quickly

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.630
roll back to a stable
version to reduce

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.460
any outages from deployments.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.920
This leads to increased
innovation and faster time

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.070
to market, resulting in happier
deployment, development,

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.930
and operations teams.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.020
With CD, oftentimes
teams embrace the concept

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.550
of immutable infrastructure.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.230
When delivering an
artifact, you can

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.350
rest assured that each
artifact is exactly the same.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.780
After initial deployment,
no additional configuration

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.060
is performed on the
VMs and containers,

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.970
thus making the fleet
completely standardized.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.700
For VMs, you can use Packer
to automatically perform

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.840
this build and versioning.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.990
And for Kubernetes and
containers, you can use Docker.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:26.230
As a result, you'll get
faster times for launch

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.150
to images and
containers as they're

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:30.940
prebaked with all software
and configurations.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.880
In practice, it looks
something like this.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.770
When we have a new binary
or configuration to deploy,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.050
we must first bake
a deployable image

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.300
based on a full
build of a server.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.640
Here we install all
runtime dependencies,

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.630
such as runtime stacks and, of
course, our application binary.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.700
You can automate this in
any way that you'd like,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.820
such as using Chef,
Puppet, or another tool.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.150
We then deploy this
image as a VM instance

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.142
in our various environments
that we test in,

00:03:57.142 --> 00:03:58.600
through to production,
where we can

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.070
deploy the same image again.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.630
If you're using
Docker containers,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.960
this is a very familiar
territory for you,

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.150
and where you can
bake images via docker

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.400
build or server-side
build services,

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.090
such as Docker Hub and Google
Container Registry build.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.070
If you're deploying
to VMs, Spinnaker

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:17.649
has Packer-based
bakeries that can support

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:19.990
the flow that I just described.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:21.430
There are other
benefits that are

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:24.220
particularly pertinent as
you're deploying to the cloud.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.260
If your Chef
recipes, for example,

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:27.910
are taking 40
minutes to complete

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:29.380
the build of a
new server, you're

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:33.040
not able to take advantage of
the elasticity of the cloud.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.500
The demand has already
come and passed.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.200
Other ops-critical functions,
such as auto-healing

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.320
and rollback images, are
similarly very difficult

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.660
if you're not using
these prebaked images.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:46.810
Another product to highlight
is Google Cloud Deploy.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.170
Google Cloud Deploy allows
users to deliver continuously

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:53.660
to Google Kubernetes Engine,
or GKE, within minutes.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.170
There are many benefits
of this managed service.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.100
First, users don't have to
own, scale, and/or maintain

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.780
CD tooling themselves.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.510
Similar to Cloud Build,
Google Cloud Deploy

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.040
is a managed service.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.110
This reduces the total
cost of ownership

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:09.730
for continuous delivery.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.770
There are no servers
to manage or patch,

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:14.560
and resources can scale
up and down automatically

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.180
to optimize cost
and performance.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.020
It also integrates with Google
Cloud Platform's Identity

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:23.410
and Access Management, and
has audit features built in.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.810
This easily enables
the securing, tracking,

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.150
and auditing of delivery
pipelines and related

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.860
activities.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.660
Google Cloud
Deploy's opinionation

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.630
aims to reduce onboarding
time and cost of ownership

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.900
by providing structure to
continuous delivery processes.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.090
You can define releases
and progress them

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:43.000
through environments such as
test, stage, and production,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.100
and there's a one-step
promotion and rollback

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:49.540
of releases via the web
console, CLI, or API.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.520
In addition, there
is built-in support

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.760
for metrics to measure and
optimize deployment success.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.520
With increased visibility,
users can gain insights

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.180
on deployment metrics and drive
objective-based SRE and DevOps

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.330
processes.

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:05.840
This is an example of an
end-to-end CI/CD workflow.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.080
The first step for
a CI/CD pipeline

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.510
is to host your code in a
source control management

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:13.900
system, such as Google
Cloud Source Repositories.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.360
Once you upload your
code to your repository,

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.700
we'll have to choose from one
of many platforms designed

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.710
to facilitate CI/CD.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.420
Many common platforms
were designed

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.790
to run all kinds of
automation and have since

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:27.490
been adjusted to support CI/CD.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:30.190
Google Cloud Build was
designed with CI/CD in mind

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.930
and is serverless,
meaning you don't

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.840
have to manage the underlying
infrastructure of your builds.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.210
You can configure
Google Cloud Build

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.030
with cloudbuild.yaml
files that live directly

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.800
in your repository.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.140
Cloud Build facilitates
CI by supporting

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.350
many different source control
services, easy integration

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.300
with Google Cloud
Services for testing,

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.220
and it supports
deploying artifacts

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.670
to various destinations,
including Artifact Registry.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:57.790
Cloud Build supports complex
builds with multiple steps--

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:00.340
for example, testing
and deployments.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.560
If you want to add
to your CI pipeline,

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.320
it's as easy as adding
an additional step to it.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.170
Take your artifacts, either
built or stored locally

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:10.720
or at your destination,
and easily deploy it

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.730
to one of our many
compute services

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:15.160
with a deployment
strategy of your choice.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.660
VALERIYA SHIN: Now
let's take a look

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:19.480
into how we can create a
deployment pipeline for Cloud

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:21.730
Run that implements
progression of code

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.850
from developer branches with
automated canary testing

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:26.500
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:29.170
Cloud Run lets you deploy
and run your applications

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.750
with little overhead or effort.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.990
Check out the Cloud Run video in
the description box below just

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:36.760
to get a quick
run-through of Cloud

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.670
Run and other compute services
available in Google Cloud

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:40.690
platform.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:44.650
In this demo, we will create
a sample Cloud Run service,

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.560
add a change on a
development branch,

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.860
merge the branch into
the mainline branch,

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:54.370
implement canary testing to rule
out any change to the Cloud Run

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:55.180
service.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:58.820
Let's start off with
preparing our environment.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:03.840
Let's go to Google
Cloud Console and login

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.370
into Google Cloud Project.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.670
In the console, we
activate Cloud Shell.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:17.000
And now let's create
environmental variables

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:20.285
and enable the needed APIs
if they're not enabled.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.365
And now let's setup the admin
roles for the service account.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:33.250
If you haven't used git
in Cloud Shell previously,

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:35.785
you can setup a username
and user email values.

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:47.630
And now we will clone and
prepare the sample repository.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.480
We will store the code
from sample repository

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:58.460
in Cloud Service Repositories.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:08:58.960
All right.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.730
Now let's move to the
Cloud Run service.

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:06.920
In Cloud Shell, we will build
and deploy the application,

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:09.880
including a service that
requires authentication.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.240
The next step is to enable
a unique URL for development

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:20.030
branches in git.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.790
Each branch is represented
by a URL that's

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:26.590
identified by the branch name.

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:29.110
Commits to the branch
trigger deployment,

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.290
and the updates are
accessible at the same URL.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:37.295
In Cloud Shell, let's
setup a trigger.

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:43.420
Now let's create a new branch
and open the sample application

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:44.260
in the Cloud Shell.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:50.060
In the sample application,
let's modify the code

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:57.210
to indicate version 1.1
instead of version 1.0.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.430
And now let's commit
the change and push

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.860
to the module repository.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.600
You can check the
progress of ongoing

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.540
build in the Cloud Build.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.360
Cloud Build can also
trigger a message via email,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.180
Slack, or other
messaging providers.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:16.101
Now let's get the unique
URL for this branch.

00:10:16.101 --> 00:10:18.330
When code is released
to production,

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:21.570
it's common to release code to
a small subset of light traffic

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:25.590
before migrating the traffic
to the new code pages.

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:28.500
Let's implement a trigger
that is activated when code is

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:30.330
committed to the main branch.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.390
The trigger deploys a code
to a unique canary URL

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:38.980
and it routes 10% of all live
traffic to the new version.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:44.750
All right, let's setup
the branch trigger

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:47.210
and now merge the
branch to the main line

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.280
and push to the
module repository.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:53.300
After the build is complete,
let's review new version.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.520
As you see, 90%
of the traffic is

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:01.790
routed to prod, 10% to canary,
and 0% to the branch revisions.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:04.430
That brings us to the end
of this video, where we've

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:08.870
been over what is CI/CD,
CI/CD in practice,

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:13.400
setting up CI/CD, deployment
strategies, and a Google Cloud

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:14.360
Console demo.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.070
We have tons of
resources and guides

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.970
around setting up CI/CD on
our documentation website.

